<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      The ONLY difference between the two products.

      This permission exists so the app can open a TCP socket to 127.0.0.1 -
      the player's own ADB daemon - and nothing else. Android has no narrower
      permission: INTERNET gates every socket, loopback included.

      The app makes no outbound connection of any kind. There is no analytics,
      no update check, no crash reporting and no remote server anywhere in this
      code base; the only socket it ever opens over IP is to the loopback
      address, and the only other socket it opens at all is Bluetooth RFCOMM,
      which is not IP. Anyone can verify that: grep the sources for Socket,
      HttpURLConnection, OkHttp or Retrofit and the only hits are the loopback
      ADB client and the RFCOMM link.

      Users who would rather not have the permission on the device at all
      should install the standard build, which does not declare it and does not
      even ship the ADB library.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.INTERNET" />

</manifest>
